WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.101
[dramatic music]

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:03.603
 Seth, Steve, congratulations.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.337
You guys have made it to this
final round of competition.

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:07.306
And that means we're sending
you back to your home forges

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.677
to recreate this iconic
weapon from history--

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:16.582
Napoleon's saber.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:17.917
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:17.984 --> 00:00:19.485
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:20.252
 Good luck.

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:22.921 line:7%
 Thanks.

00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:24.423 line:7%
[rock music]

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.961
SETH: I'm back home at my home
forge in Gautier, Mississippi,

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:30.597
and I'm going to be working
on Napoleon's saber.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.099
Round one, I let the
pressure get to my head,

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:34.467
and I didn't meet parameters.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:36.903
So I'm really going to have
to make sure that I don't

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:41.307
get flustered and make this
thing beautiful and functional

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.675
as well.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.711
I decided to go with 5160.

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:48.314
It is a great spring steel.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:51.684
But I'm looking at the
blade, and I think I have

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:53.419
a little too much curvature.

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:58.024
I was at the 3-inch mark, so I
had to put it back in the forge

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.560
and knock it down.

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:04.097
I knocked it down
a little too far.

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:05.999
Time to go back in
the forge again.

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:08.735
It's kind of like a
cat-and-mouse game.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:11.504
It's a little over an inch.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:14.240
Well, it's just one
of those things.

00:01:14.306 --> 00:01:18.178
It's back and forth and back
and forth, and I finally got it.

00:01:22.014 --> 00:01:23.015
STEVEN: I'm back at home.

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:24.417
I'm in my comfort zone.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.120
The lack of power tools could
put me at a disadvantage,

00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:31.424
so I need to plan wisely.

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:34.159
I've got a nice bar of 5160.

00:01:34.226 --> 00:01:36.895
It's going to make a
good, strong sword.

00:01:36.962 --> 00:01:39.699
It is super hard to hammer.

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:41.567
And I'm going to have
to just get after it

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.303
and know that day one
is going to be tough.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.173
But I'm going to
have to suck it up.

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:49.775
We're going to be here a while.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:54.580
For the pommel, I'm using
1-inch solid, hot-rolled steel.

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:57.082
I've got a lot of
metal to move, and I'm

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:58.785
a little bit worried
that I might not

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:01.221
get all this metal moved today.

00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:08.094
Finally, I've got this
thing where I need it.

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:11.063
We're at the correct length,
the correct thickness.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.466
Today is a success.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.535
All right, feels pretty good.

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:19.471 line:7%
SETH: It's the start of day two.

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:21.607
It's time to move on to the
heat treatment of the blade.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:27.480
The idea is to get as even
a heat on this as possible.

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:30.750
And like Napoleon, I
will divide and conquer.

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:37.656
I don't feel no ticks.

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:39.858
I don't feel no tings.

00:02:39.925 --> 00:02:42.594
I have a hardened blade.

00:02:42.628 --> 00:02:45.732
It's time to do a rough
bit up of the wood part

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.667
of the handle and the pommel.

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:52.972
And this is going to
taper down to the brass.

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.840
That's the idea.

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:57.210
Now, is it going to work?

00:02:57.277 --> 00:03:00.245
I go to try to bend it, and
it bends just a little bit

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:01.614
on the first go-round.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:02.648
And I'm like, all right.

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:03.515
All right.

00:03:03.582 --> 00:03:04.951
This looks like
it's going to work.

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:07.586
I go to try to bend
it one more time.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.321
Oh!

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:08.988
Mm!

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:10.256
Broke the brass.

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:11.924
That was my biggest
piece of brass

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:13.526
that I could have
used for it, so

00:03:13.592 --> 00:03:15.528
that's kind of down the drain.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.931
At this point, I don't
really know what to do.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:21.134
But I look down at
the floor, and I'm

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.002
looking at the deer
antlers that I have.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.104
And I notice the
curvature in them.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:26.773
No one says that
it has to be made

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.075
out of brass, copper, or gold.

00:03:29.141 --> 00:03:32.711
As long as I hollow it out,
and it has a nice taper to it,

00:03:32.778 --> 00:03:38.584
and it fits the hand,
why not a deer antler?

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.553 line:7%
We got a fit.

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:42.788 line:0%
STEVEN: Today we'll be
quenching the blade.

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:45.391
The quench is a moment
that can go horribly wrong.

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:49.495
I've got to get the entire blade
one nice, uniform temperature.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:52.298
It's not going to be very easy
in the short forge that I have.

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:55.434
So what I plan on doing
is building an oven

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.003
at the backside of the forge.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.805
This oven's going to
keep my point warm

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.407
but not let it cool off.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:04.276
Should be a good help.

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:07.146
So I've got the forge turned
up as high as it'll go.

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:10.082
I've got my little butane
torch in the front.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.584
All right, here we go.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.194
We're straight.

00:04:22.261 --> 00:04:24.631
The beautiful sound
of a skating file--

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:26.465
hard, hard, hard.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:27.600
 How'd the quench go?

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:29.302
 Quench went good, I think--

00:04:29.369 --> 00:04:34.240
no bends, no warps, got
it out, file skated on it.

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:35.675
 I'm ready to see it finished.

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:37.910
 Give me a little while,
and we'll have that done, OK?

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:38.778
 OK.

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:39.811
- Thanks for checking.
- No problem.

00:04:39.845 --> 00:04:40.313
 Love you.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.882 line:7%
 Love you too.

00:04:42.948 --> 00:04:45.851 line:0%
SETH: The closer and closer
I get towards the final shape

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.954 line:0%
of it, I'm feeling it in my
hand, kind of twirling it

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:50.223 line:7%
a little bit--

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.457 line:0%
not quite as good as Doug
Marcaida will be doing,

00:04:52.524 --> 00:04:55.561 line:7%
but feels good in the hand.

00:04:55.627 --> 00:04:57.029
Becoming a "Forged
in Fire" champion

00:04:57.063 --> 00:05:01.634
would be a great way to show
people that I can do this

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:03.736
and would be one
step closer for me

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:05.571
to becoming a
full-time bladesmith,

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:09.107
because this is the
dream right here.

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:11.143
I am done.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.079 line:7%
I couldn't be happier.

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:15.314 line:7%
STEVEN: It's the last day.

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:18.251
I tempered in the blade
yesterday in the oven,

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:20.519
but I felt like I
maybe half did the job.

00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:23.088
So I want to temper
my blade with my torch

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:25.258
to give me peace of mind.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:26.826
There is risk
involved in the torch

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:31.230
temper, which means that I could
lose the hardness of the blade.

00:05:31.296 --> 00:05:33.866
I should see a nice
blue up toward the top

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:40.239
and a little straw moving down
toward the middle of the blade.

00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:42.608
I think that Seth
is going to try

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.411
to design a blade
that's going to be much

00:05:45.477 --> 00:05:47.279
prettier and sharper than mine.

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:49.749
But I sacrificed a
little bit of sharpness

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:53.486
to get a little more meat
behind that cutting edge.

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:55.588
I'm in at about 3
pounds right now.

00:05:55.654 --> 00:05:59.158
It's going to cut, but it's
not going to be a razor blade.

00:05:59.191 --> 00:06:00.793
It doesn't feel horribly heavy.

00:06:00.859 --> 00:06:01.961
It really doesn't.

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:02.929
Put it right there.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:06.265
There's one more thing to do.

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:07.133
It will kill.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:09.035
MAN: It will kill.

00:06:09.102 --> 00:06:10.769
DOUG MARCAIDA: All right,
bladesmiths, to find out

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:12.939
what kind of lethal damage
your weapon will do,

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:16.809
I'm gonna to deliver slashes
and thrusts on this pig carcass.

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:17.777
Seth, you're up first.

00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:18.878
You ready for this?

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:20.178
 Let's do it.

00:06:20.245 --> 00:06:22.615
[rock music]

00:06:23.215 --> 00:06:28.721
[blade impacts]

00:06:41.134 --> 00:06:43.569
 All right, Seth, from
the snotter to the trotter,

00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:45.504
every cut with this,
as you can see,

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:49.441
offers a very deep,
lacerating cut.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:51.644
The only issue is
that your blade did

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.179
pick up a little bit of a bend.

00:06:53.245 --> 00:06:57.850
But overall, sir, with the way
this weapon moves and feels,

00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:59.117
it will kill.

00:06:59.184 --> 00:07:00.185
 Thank you very much.

00:07:00.252 --> 00:07:01.220
DOUG MARCAIDA: Good job.

00:07:01.286 --> 00:07:02.321
All right, Steven, your turn.

00:07:02.388 --> 00:07:03.155
You ready, sir?

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:04.956
 Yes, sir.

00:07:05.023 --> 00:07:07.193
[rock music]

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:13.833
[blade impacts]

00:07:36.054 --> 00:07:39.125
 All right, Steven, let's
talk about your saber here.

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:40.893
It's a heavy, unbalanced sword.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.263
The minute you pick up, it
wants to pull straight down.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:45.664
It's hard to wield.

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:49.401
But the weight of your sword
here will definitely bash

00:07:49.468 --> 00:07:53.271
in and cut, so it will kill.

00:07:53.338 --> 00:07:54.707
 Thank you.

00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:56.675
 Well, bladesmiths, a saber's
supposed to be a sturdy weapon,

00:07:56.709 --> 00:07:59.278
so let's see how yours hold
up on our strength test--

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:01.847
the ram skull chop.

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:05.284
Now, I'm going to take each of
your sabers and, with no mercy

00:08:05.351 --> 00:08:07.753
at all, bash them
into this ram's skull.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.556
It's not only going to test the
overall strength of your knife

00:08:10.589 --> 00:08:12.024
but the heat treat on your edge.

00:08:12.091 --> 00:08:14.059
Now, I don't really care what
your swords do to the skulls.

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:16.162
I want to see what the
skulls do to your swords.

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:17.196
Now, Seth, you're up first.

00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:17.997
You ready to go?

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:20.532
 Swing it good.

00:08:20.599 --> 00:08:22.768
[rock music]

00:08:22.802 --> 00:08:25.705
[grunting]

00:08:32.378 --> 00:08:33.912
 All right, Seth,
your edge held up.

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:36.916
But we do have a bigger bend in
the blade than we had before.

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:40.619
Your guard is now
mobile, and both

00:08:40.685 --> 00:08:42.387
your bolsters came off as well.

00:08:42.454 --> 00:08:44.123
So this mechanical
connection right in here

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.725
failed, but the connection
between your blade and

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:48.694
your handle is still sturdy.

00:08:48.761 --> 00:08:50.429
You took some damage,
but you survived.

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:51.230
Good job.

00:08:51.297 --> 00:08:52.431
 Thank you, sir.

00:08:52.497 --> 00:08:55.367
 Steve, after seeing
that, how do you feel?

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:56.334
 I don't know.

00:08:56.368 --> 00:08:57.068
Let's see.

00:08:57.135 --> 00:08:58.871
[rock music]

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:05.678
[clanging]

00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:08.881
Daggone.

00:09:11.951 --> 00:09:13.985
 Well, Steve, we obviously
have a problem here.

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:16.522
You got a very coarse
grain structure in here,

00:09:16.588 --> 00:09:18.223
so I don't know if there
was enough tempering

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:20.492
done on this 5160 blade.

00:09:20.526 --> 00:09:22.528
I mean, one strike
is all it took,

00:09:22.594 --> 00:09:24.130
and it snapped it right in half.

00:09:24.196 --> 00:09:25.331
So that's obviously a problem.

00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:30.702
 Well, Steve, there's
nothing left to test.

00:09:30.769 --> 00:09:32.804
Your blade has suffered
a catastrophic failure

00:09:32.871 --> 00:09:35.040
and can no longer continue
in this competition.

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:36.675
And for that reason,
I have to ask

00:09:36.741 --> 00:09:39.946
you to please exit the forge.

00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:41.113
STEVEN: I'm upset.

00:09:41.179 --> 00:09:43.649
I'm very disappointed that
the blade broke like that.

00:09:43.682 --> 00:09:45.584
I'm not coming
home as a champion,

00:09:45.651 --> 00:09:47.786
but I'm coming home
a whole lot smarter.

00:09:47.853 --> 00:09:50.522
I've made some friends
that I'll have forever.

00:09:50.555 --> 00:09:52.959
I would do this again,
no questions asked.

00:09:56.561 --> 00:09:58.464
 Well, Seth, this
is a competition

00:09:58.531 --> 00:09:59.665
where the best blade wins.

00:09:59.698 --> 00:10:01.767
Your blade survived
this strength test.

00:10:01.834 --> 00:10:03.802
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion,

00:10:03.869 --> 00:10:07.539
and that's a title that comes
with a check for $10,000.

00:10:07.573 --> 00:10:08.741
How do you feel?

00:10:08.808 --> 00:10:10.375
 Surreal right now.

00:10:10.442 --> 00:10:11.377
 Well, good job, brother.

00:10:11.443 --> 00:10:12.744
You're a champion.

00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:14.980
SETH: I could not be more
excited about this experience.

00:10:15.046 --> 00:10:18.217
This competition has been some
of the most fun of my life.

00:10:18.284 --> 00:10:20.386
The money's going to do
some things for the shop.

00:10:20.419 --> 00:10:23.355
And my wife and my
daughter are going

00:10:23.422 --> 00:10:25.524
to go on a real nice trip.

00:10:25.591 --> 00:10:26.826
 Good job, Seth.

00:10:26.892 --> 00:10:28.594
 My name is Seth Borries,
and I'm a "Forged in Fire"

00:10:28.661 --> 00:10:30.262
champion.

